(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What post-surgical advice is given to a patient prone to keloids?","choices":["Wear properly fitting shoes and socks and use padding inside the shoe","Avoid stretching the immature scar, avoid hot baths, keep the wound clean, and avoid body piercings","Massage the scar firmly twice daily, take warm baths, and expose the site to sunlight","Reposition every two hours, manage incontinence, and apply barrier creams"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hot baths can aggravate surgery-induced inflammation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is intralesional methotrexate used for in keratoacanthoma, and how does it work?","choices":["Given topically over several months; it slows the turnover of sebocytes within the enlarged sebaceous gland","Given before excision to reduce the size of the lesion; it inhibits deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis in actively dividing cells","Given after excision in order to prevent recurrence; it inhibits fibroblast proliferation within the healing wound bed","Given instead of excision in frail patients; it induces microvascular thrombosis within the vessels feeding the lesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a chemotherapeutic agent used as a surgical adjunct. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the standard of care for removing an epidermoid cyst, and what is the option for a small one?","choices":["Removal of the entire capsule when the cyst is not inflamed; a small one can be punched and emptied","Simple drainage of the keratin contents through a stab incision, leaving the capsule in place to scar down on its own","Elliptical excision with five-millimetre margins in every case, with the specimen sent for histopathological analysis","Scissor excision at the level of the stalk followed by electrodesiccation, with no local anesthesia required"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the capsule is the part that matters, and removing all of it is the standard of care. A cyst of one to three centimetres can instead be treated with a punch incision and removal of the contents. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What does sebaceous hyperplasia look like?","choices":["Multiple skin-coloured papules of one to two millimetres on the eyelids and upper cheeks","A firm nodule with a brown halo and a scaly raised centre, usually on a leg","Soft whitish-yellow papules with a central umbilication, on the face","Soft yellow cholesterol plaques on the medial eyelids, without any umbilication"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — single or multiple, two to nine millimetres, and a very small globule of sebum can sometimes be expressed from the centre. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What determines the management of a cutaneous horn?","choices":["The cosmetic concern of the patient alone, since the underlying lesion is benign in essentially every case","The underlying etiology, with excision to the standard for the tumour type and location if malignant","The height of the horn itself, with excision reserved for any projection measuring over one centimetre","The patient's age at presentation, with watchful observation preferred in anyone over the age of seventy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — you treat what is at the base, not the horn. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is a telangiectasia?","choices":["A congenital dilation of dermal capillaries through the full depth of the dermis, present at birth","An acquired proliferation of capillaries and venules forming a deep red papule under five millimetres","A rapidly growing vascular papule that arises in response to trauma or hormonal change","A permanently dilated capillary under one millimetre, blanchable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an acquired vascular lesion, single or in groups, with a central punctum. It may be primary or secondary and is associated with numerous diseases. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What should a patient be told about cherry angiomas?","choices":["New lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them","They are premalignant and each one should be biopsied before removal","They indicate a lipid disorder and warrant fasting cholesterol testing","They will fade within a year, so no intervention is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary unless they bother the patient. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"How does a plexiform neurofibroma differ from a cutaneous one?","choices":["It is confined to the brain and spinal cord and never involves peripheral tissue","It is a pigmented macule rather than a tumour, and it grows with the child","It is a tumour in the tissue covering the nerves, occurs anywhere except brain and spinal cord, and is large and extensive","It is a discrete mass arising from a single peripheral nerve, sits just above or under the skin, and stays well circumscribed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and it may be locally invasive. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is the difference between a sinus and a fistula?","choices":["A sinus follows a surgical wound; a fistula is always congenital in origin","A sinus is a blind track; a fistula is a track connecting two epithelium-lined surfaces","A sinus connects two epithelium-lined surfaces; a fistula is a blind-ending track","A sinus is lined by keratin; a fistula is lined by granulation tissue only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both usually arise from a preceding abscess. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What do syringomas look like, and where do they sit?","choices":["Light tan to brown macules over five millimetres, appearing in the first year of life","Multiple skin-coloured, pink or brown papules of one to two millimetres, most often on the eyelids and upper cheeks","Multiple whitish-yellow papules of two to nine millimetres with central umbilication, most often on the face","Soft yellow cholesterol plaques, most often on the medial eyelids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the periorbital location is the recognisable part. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What should a keloid-prone patient be advised to avoid?","choices":["Cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing, and body piercings generally","Sun exposure during peak hours, and tanning beds at any time","Tight footwear and walking barefoot without socks","Oral contraceptives and any medication affecting the liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — prevention is called the most important treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the dimple sign, and which lesion shows it?","choices":["The lesion retracts beneath the skin surface with lateral compression; dermatofibroma","The lesion blanches completely under direct downward pressure; cherry angioma","The lesion transmits a wave-like fluid shift on palpation; pilonidal abscess","The lesion expresses pasty material through a central pore; epidermoid cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion retracts inward when pinched, which is the dimple sign. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the defining physical finding of a keloid?","choices":["It stays confined within the margins of the original wound","It retracts beneath the skin on lateral compression","It blanches completely with direct pressure","It extends beyond the margins of the original wound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that single feature separates it from a hypertrophic scar. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What should be screened for in a patient with xanthelasma, and why does it matter?","choices":["Liver disease; it may signify increased risk of variceal bleeding","Hyperlipidemia; it may signify increased risk of cardiac disease","Diabetes mellitus; it may signify increased risk of peripheral neuropathy","Thyroid dysfunction; it may signify increased risk of periorbital myxoedema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is associated with lipid disorders. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which site of intertriginous freckling is NOT listed as a diagnostic criterion?","choices":["The inguinal region","Both the axilla and the inguinal region","Under the breasts","The axilla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it may be observed there, but the criteria name axilla and groin. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What is xanthelasma made of, and where does it sit?","choices":["A collection of mucin extruded from a joint space, most commonly over a distal finger joint","A crowding of sebocytes within an enlarged gland, most commonly on the face","A collection of lipid-laden macrophages forming soft yellow cholesterol plaques, most commonly on the medial eyelids","A collection of keratin within an enclosed epithelial sac, most commonly on the face, scalp and trunk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and it is asymptomatic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Where do calluses typically develop, and what happens if the process is acute and severe?","choices":["On the palms of the hands or the balls of the feet; a blister forms","In the fourth-to-fifth toe web space, where the skin macerates; an abscess forms","On the dorsal and lateral fifth toe, over the joint; a deep ulcer forms","Over the sacrum and other bony prominences; a black eschar forms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a blister is the acute end of the same friction process. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In what circumstance do hypertrophic scars characteristically occur?","choices":["When a wound is left to heal by secondary intention rather than closed","When the patient has dark skin, which is strongly associated with them","When a wound becomes infected during the proliferative phase of healing","When scars cross joints or skin creases at a right angle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and they develop soon after surgery rather than months later. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis for a pyogenic granuloma?","choices":["Keloid, hypertrophic scar and foreign-body granuloma, the fibrous group","Cherry angioma, malignant melanoma and squamous cell carcinoma","Nevus flammeus and infantile hemangioma, the two congenital vascular lesions","Milia, xanthelasma and basal cell carcinoma, the periorbital group"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — two malignancies, which is why treatment is often taken up. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"When do café au lait spots appear, and how do they change?","choices":["Usually after the age of fifty, on sun-exposed skin; they darken with further exposure","Usually in the first weeks of life; they proliferate and then involute","Usually at birth or in the first year; they grow in proportion to the child","Usually at puberty; they increase in number and size with age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What should the history cover in a patient with spider angiomas?","choices":["Pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol history, and medications that carry a risk of liver damage","Birth history, feeding and weight gain, and any family history of vascular birthmarks","Footwear, occupation and use of tools or sports equipment","Immobility, incontinence and nutritional status"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesion can be a marker of estrogen excess or liver disease. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"When is a lipoma treated?","choices":["When cosmetically deforming, or the diagnosis is uncertain","Always at diagnosis, because of the risk of transformation to liposarcoma","Only once it exceeds ten centimetres, regardless of symptoms or appearance","Only after a trial of intralesional corticosteroid has failed to shrink it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an enlarged deforming mass or diagnostic uncertainty can be treated surgically with excision. Asymptomatic tumours can simply be observed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis for a dermatofibroma?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, amelanotic melanoma and molluscum contagiosum","Cystic acne, lipoma, neurofibroma, keratoacanthoma and basal cell carcinoma","Milia, xanthelasma and basal cell carcinoma, all of which sit around the eye","Basal cell carcinoma, hypertrophic scar, cutaneous melanoma and keratoacanthoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — two of the four are malignancies, which is why it matters. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the natural history of a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["It enlarges continuously for months to years and does not regress","It grows rapidly for six to eight weeks and then involutes over three to six months","It is present at birth, grows with the child, and never involutes","It remains stable and then regresses, flattening with time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why observation is reasonable. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What causes a pressure injury?","choices":["Disruption of the skin over the coccyx drawing in hair and debris, with follicular plugging that prevents drainage","Loss of the control mechanisms that normally regulate tissue repair, producing overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue","Unrelieved pressure damaging underlying tissue, generally soft tissue compressed between a bony prominence and an external surface for a prolonged time","Repeated friction over a broad area of skin, producing diffuse hyperkeratosis with a poorly defined margin and no central keratin core at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the injury ranges from intact skin to ulcers reaching bone. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is an acrochordon, anatomically?","choices":["A cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis, filled with keratin","A localised overgrowth of fat cells within the subcutaneous tissue","A fibroepithelial pedunculated papilloma, with a narrow stalk and a broad tip","A benign neoplasm of eccrine ducts, appearing in crops around the eyes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why it can be snipped off at the stalk. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Where does a digital mucous cyst sit, and what can it do to the nail?","choices":["Over the distal interphalangeal joint; it may groove the nail","Over the proximal interphalangeal joint; pressure on the nail bed may cause pitting","Along the lateral nail fold; it causes retraction of the cuticle over months","Under the free edge of the nail; it causes complete separation of the nail plate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a translucent skin-coloured papule on the distal aspect of the digit, over the proximal nail matrix or the nail bed. Pressure on the matrix causes a longitudinal groove. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"What is the earliest sign of an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["A flat well-circumscribed blanchable patch that darkens with crying, fever or overheating","A pink to erythematous irregular macule that becomes more noticeable when the baby cries","A bright red exophytic papule with a moist surface that bleeds after minor trauma","Blanching of the involved skin, then fine telangiectasias, then a red or crimson macule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — about half are present at birth as a prominent cutaneous mark. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What must a patient be told before electing cosmetic removal of a benign lesion?","choices":["The likelihood that the lesion will become malignant if left","The risk of pigmentary changes and the chance of recurrence","The need for lifelong surveillance of the site afterwards","The requirement for histopathological analysis in every case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both must be fully explained beforehand. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"How are corns and calluses managed?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroid injection, with silicone gel sheeting worn daily","Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen, repeated at two-week intervals until resolved","Elliptical surgical excision with five-millimetre margins, sent for histology","Padding and avoiding poorly fitting footwear, with over-the-counter keratolytic products"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — remove the pressure first, then thin the keratin. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 9"}]};
